# Introduction # {#sctn-intro}

The FIDO Credential Manager Reference Architecture (CMRA) defines a standardized architecture for [=Credential Manager=] applications — commonly referred to as digital wallets — that are eligible for FIDO certification. A [=Credential Manager=] is a secure, user-controlled application that enables individuals (i.e., [=Holders|holders=]) to request [=Verifiable Digital Credentials|verifiable digital credentials=] from trusted [=Credential Issuers|issuers=], store those credentials on their device, and present them to [=Verifiers=], for use by [=Relying Parties=], in a manner that prioritizes privacy and security. As digital identity ecosystems mature and proliferate across jurisdictions, the need for a common reference architecture becomes critical. Without a shared baseline, implementations risk fragmentation, inconsistent security postures, and poor interoperability between [=Credential Issuers|credential issuers=], [=Holders|holders=], and [=Verifiers|verifiers=].

This document addresses that gap by establishing a set of requirements and architectural patterns that a [=Credential Manager=] MUST implement to obtain FIDO certification. It serves as a blueprint for a general-purpose [=Credential Manager=] capable of holding and presenting Digital Credentials across a variety of use cases — from government-issued identity documents to age verification and professional [=Attestations|attestations=]. Specifically, this document serves three purposes:

- **Explains the architecture:** It details the system components and their interactions, providing the context necessary to understand the certification requirements.
- **Guides implementation:** It acts as a common reference for implementers building [=Credential Manager=] applications, defining the components, interfaces, protocols, and security posture required for conformance.
- **Supports certification:** It establishes the criteria against which [=Credential Manager=] implementations will be evaluated during the FIDO certification process.

The audience for this document includes [=Credential Manager Providers|Credential Manager providers=] (wallet and app developers), [=Credential Issuers=], [=Verifiers=] seeking to verify credentials, conformity assessment bodies, and platform or device vendors whose infrastructure supports credential management operations.


# Terminology and Definitions # {#sctn-terminology}
<!-- Section owner: All authors as they write their sections -->

The following terms are used throughout this document:

: <dfn>Attestation</dfn>
:: A cryptographically verifiable statement about the properties of a component or entity in the ecosystem (e.g., a Wallet Attestation proving the authenticity of a [=Credential Manager=] instance).

: <dfn>Credential Issuer</dfn>
:: An entity that verifies attributes about a subject and issues signed credentials to a [=Credential Manager=] for storage.

: <dfn>Credential Manager</dfn>
:: An application installed on or accessible from a [=User Device=] that implements the core logic for requesting, storing, managing, and presenting Digital Credentials. Informally referred to as a wallet.

: <dfn export>Credential Manager Attestation</dfn>
:: A cryptographic proof issued by the [=Credential Manager Provider=] that attests to the authenticity and integrity of a [=Credential Manager=] instance, enabling [=Credential Issuers|Issuers=] and [=Relying Parties=] to verify they are communicating with a genuine application.

: <dfn>Credential Manager Provider</dfn>
:: The organization responsible for developing, distributing, and maintaining a [=Credential Manager=] application and its associated backend services.

: <dfn>Holder</dfn>
:: The [=User=] who possesses and controls one or more credentials within their [=Credential Manager=].

: <dfn>Presentation</dfn>
:: The act of a [=Credential Manager=] transmitting one or more credentials (or selected attributes thereof) to a [=Relying Party=] in response to a verified request.

: <dfn>Presentation Request</dfn>
:: A message from a [=Verifier=] specifying the credentials or attributes it requires, along with metadata such as the [=Verifier=] and/or [=Relying Party|Relying Party's=] identity and the purpose of the request.

: <dfn>Proximity Flow</dfn>
:: A credential [=Presentation|presentation=] flow in which the [=User Device=] and the [=Verifier=] are physically co-located and exchange data over short-range communication technologies (e.g., NFC, BLE).

: <dfn lt="Relying Party|RP">Relying Party</dfn> (<abbr>RP</abbr>)
:: An entity that relies on the [=Presentation|presentation=] of credentials from a [=Credential Manager=] to make authorization or access control decisions.

: <dfn>Remote Flow</dfn>
:: A credential [=Presentation|presentation=] flow in which the [=Credential Manager=] and the [=Verifier=] communicate over the internet, either on the same device or across devices.

: <dfn lt="Secure Cryptographic Application|SCA">Secure Cryptographic Application</dfn> (<abbr>SCA</abbr>)
:: An application or interface layer that manages critical cryptographic assets by interfacing with the [=Secure Cryptographic Device=].

: <dfn lt="Secure Cryptographic Device|SCD">Secure Cryptographic Device</dfn> (<abbr>SCD</abbr>)
:: A tamper-resistant device or environment that is linked to and used by the [=Credential Manager=] through the [=Secure Cryptographic Application=] to protect critical cryptographic assets (e.g., private keys) and to securely execute cryptographic functions.

: <dfn>Selective Disclosure</dfn>
:: A capability allowing a [=User=] to present only a subset of the attributes contained in a credential, rather than the full credential payload.

: <dfn>User</dfn>
:: A natural person who installs, controls, and operates a [=Credential Manager=] to manage and present their Digital Credentials.

: <dfn>User Device</dfn>
:: The hardware, operating system, and software environment on which a [=Credential Manager=] instance is hosted and executed.

: <dfn>Verifiable Digital Credential</dfn>
:: A set of attributes about a subject, digitally signed by a [=Credential Issuer=], cryptographically bound to a device, held within a [=Credential Manager=], that conforms to a recognized credential format (e.g., mdoc [[ISO-18013-5]], SD-JWT VC [[SD-JWT-VC]]).

: <dfn>Verifier</dfn>
:: An entity that receives and cryptographically validates credentials or [=Presentations|presentations=] from a [=Credential Manager=]. Often synonymous with [=Relying Party=] in [=Presentation|presentation=] flows, but is a discrete entity/component.


# Conformance # {#sctn-conformance}

As well as sections marked as non-normative, all authoring guidelines, diagrams, examples, and notes in this document are non-normative. Everything else in this document is normative.

The key words _MUST_, _MUST NOT_, _REQUIRED_, _SHALL_, _SHALL NOT_, _SHOULD_, _SHOULD NOT_, _RECOMMENDED_, _MAY_, and _OPTIONAL_ in this document are to be interpreted as described in [[!RFC2119]].


# High Level Architecture # {#sctn-high-level-architecture}

## Introduction ## {#high-level-arch-intro}

_This section provides a broad overview of the [=Credential Manager=] ecosystem's core components, their interfaces, and the overall design principles. It is structured as follows:_

- [[#design-principles]] discusses the design principles that guide the architecture described in this document.
- [[#reference-architecture]] presents an overview of the ecosystem's architecture, focusing on the components that make up a [=Credential Manager=] and the interfaces between a [=Credential Manager=] and other entities, as well as the protocols used on these interfaces.
- [[#credential-presentation-flows]] discusses the different credential [=Presentation|presentation=] flows enabled by this architecture, including mechanisms for both proximity and remote [=Presentation|presentation=].
- [[#scd-architecture-types]] discusses the different architecture types that may be used to implement a [=Secure Cryptographic Device=] within a [=Credential Manager=].
- [[#state-diagrams]] presents state diagrams for the main entities and components in the ecosystem.

## Design Principles ## {#design-principles}

_This section establishes the design principles that guide this Reference Architecture, ensuring the resulting ecosystem is user-centric, accessible, interoperable, private, and secure by design._

### User-Centricity ### {#user-centricity}

The [=Verifiable Digital Credentials=] (VDC) ecosystem prioritizes user-centricity as a core design principle. This means placing user needs and experience at the forefront of every design decision. The [=Credential Manager=] SHOULD be intuitive and easy to use, with seamless integration into existing identity verification use cases. [=Users=] MUST be able to exercise control over their credentials — including the ability to view, present, and delete them — with transparent information about what attributes are being shared and with whom, to include logs, accessible only to the [=Holder|holder=], that detail credential sharing transactions.

The [=Credential Manager=] MUST make it straightforward for [=Users=] to understand the implications of a [=Presentation Request|presentation request=] before they authorize the sharing of attributes. This includes clearly communicating the identity of the requesting [=Verifier=] and/or [=Relying Party=], the specific attributes being requested, and the purpose of the request where available. Additionally, the [=Credential Manager=] SHOULD be accessible and inclusive, catering to users with varying technical backgrounds and abilities. By prioritizing user-centricity, the FIDO [=Credential Manager=] ecosystem fosters trust and encourages widespread adoption, ultimately empowering [=Users=] with secure and convenient digital credential management.

### Accessibility ### {#accessibility}

Accessibility of all user-facing components in the [=Credential Manager=] ecosystem — including the [=Credential Manager=] application itself, enrollment and issuance interfaces, and credential [=Presentation|presentation=] interfaces — is essential to ensure these digital tools are inclusive by design. Accessibility is not only a matter of regulatory compliance but also a fundamental condition for equal access, user trust, and widespread adoption across all segments of the population, including persons with disabilities.

[=Credential Managers=] and related user-facing components SHOULD align with applicable accessibility standards, such as the W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines [[WCAG22]] and platform-specific accessibility APIs (e.g., iOS VoiceOver, Android TalkBack). It is crucial that accessibility is integrated into:

- Technical specifications and protocol design
- User interface design and interaction patterns
- Testing processes and conformance criteria
- Procurement and certification requirements

Implementers SHOULD ensure ongoing monitoring, effective feedback mechanisms, and continuous improvement of accessibility practices. This requires collaboration with users, accessibility experts, and organizations representing persons with disabilities, to guarantee that [=Credential Manager=] applications remain usable, effective, and inclusive for all.

#### Requirements #### {#accessibility-requirements}

: **Issuance - Issuing Entity**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST present to the [=User|user=] who the issuing entity is when an issuance process is initiated.

: **Issuance - Consent**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST capture the [=User|user's=] consent prior to conveying any information (e.g., biometrics, document images, attributes) to the [=Credential Issuer|issuer=] during an issuance process.

: **Issuance - Notice**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST provide notice and plain language information about the issuance processes that occur within the [=Credential Manager=] user interface. Where the [=Credential Issuer|issuer=] makes such information available, the [=Credential Manager=] MUST provide the [=User|user=] with information about alternative methods available to them during the issuance process (e.g., accessibility options, alternative verification paths). The [=Credential Manager=] SHOULD provide information prior to initiating the issuance process about the documents, steps, and information that will be required to complete the issuance steps that are executed within the [=Credential Manager=] user interface.

: **Presentation - Purpose**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST convey the purpose of the credential request to the [=User|user=] if the [=Relying Party=] and [=Presentation|presentation=] protocols provide it.

: **Presentation - Attribute Display**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST display to the [=User|user=] all the attributes that have been requested by the [=Relying Party=].

: **Presentation - Consent**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST acquire consent from the [=User|user=] prior to the [=Presentation|presentation=] of any attributes to the [=RP=]. The consent step MUST require active user engagement and MUST be done prior to user authentication/verification to release the attributes.

: **Transaction History**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST provide the [=User|user=] with a transaction log that indicates which credentials and attributes they have presented, to what [=Relying Parties|relying parties=], and when. This transaction log MUST be available only to the [=User|user=]. The [=Credential Manager=] MAY truncate this log in the user interface (e.g., based on recency or number of transactions) for ease of presentation, but MUST provide a means for the [=User|user=] to access the complete transaction history.

: **Credential Deletion**
:: The [=Credential Manager=] MUST provide the [=User|user=] with a means to delete stored credentials from their device. The deletion process SHOULD provide the [=User|user=] with a clear indication that the credential will no longer be usable once deleted and that the [=User|user=] may be required to go through a re-issuance process to regain access to the credential. This process MUST delete all traces of the credential from the [=User|user's=] device including any metadata that could reveal information about the deleted credential or its use, and, at the option of the [=User|user=], activity log information. The process MUST NOT require approval by the Issuing Authority and MUST be able to be completed while the [=User|user's=] device is offline.

### Interoperability ### {#interoperability}
<!-- Section owner: Rene Leveille -->

<p class="issue">
Placeholder: [=Credential Managers=] SHOULD function seamlessly across implementations, platforms, and jurisdictions, using standardized protocols so that any conformant [=Verifier=] can interact with any conformant [=Credential Manager=].
</p>

### Privacy by Design ### {#privacy-by-design}

The [=Credential Manager=] architecture embodies the principle of privacy by design. The protection of user data is a fundamental pillar of the architecture, not an afterthought. The following privacy principles guide the design:

- **Data Minimization:** The architecture ensures that [=Relying Parties=] can request and receive only the specific attributes they need to fulfill a transaction. [=Credential Managers=] MUST NOT transmit attributes beyond those explicitly authorized by the [=User=] for a given [=Presentation|presentation=].
- **[=Selective Disclosure=]:** The [=Credential Manager=] empowers [=Users=] with control over what data is presented. Where credential formats support it, [=Users=] SHOULD be able to disclose individual attributes or subsets of attributes from a credential rather than presenting the entire credential payload.
- **Transparency:** The [=Credential Manager=] MUST provide clear explanations to [=Users=] about what data is being requested, by whom, and for what stated purpose. To the extent that [=Verifier|verifier=] requests enable it, [=Credential Managers=] MUST notify the [=User|user=] of what attributes are mandatory or optional for a given transaction. [=Credential Managers=] MUST NOT present any credentials without the explicit consent of the [=User=].

<p class="issue">
Are we allowing mandatory vs. optional attribute requests? Need to confirm whether Users can deselect attributes they do not want to share or whether mandatory vs. optional attributes exist in this model (to minimize data, verifiers request what they need rather than optional attributes).
</p>

- **Unlinkability:** [=Verifiable Digital Credentials=], if not implemented with appropriate measures, could be misused to track users across [=Relying Parties=], [=Credential Issuers=], or other ecosystem participants. [=Credential Managers=] SHOULD support mechanisms that prevent colluding parties from correlating a [=User|User's=] activities across transactions. Examples of such mechanisms can include batch issuance, one-time-use [=Presentations|presentations=], or cryptographic unlinkability techniques.

<p class="issue">
Do we want to make a privacy risk assessment a requirement, and is this the place to do that?
</p>

By making privacy a cornerstone from the beginning, the FIDO [=Credential Manager=] ecosystem aims to foster trust and protect the fundamental rights of its [=Users=].

